# Cloudflare Pages headers. Consumed at deploy; never served as a file.
#
# WHY THIS IS SAFE, AND WHAT WOULD MAKE IT UNSAFE
#
# Cloudflare Pages' default for /shared/* is `max-age=14400, must-revalidate` —
# four hours, then a conditional request per asset per visitor. On a class set
# of 30 Chromebooks opening a lesson at the same moment that is 30 round trips
# for bytes that have not changed, on school wifi, before anything renders.
#
# `immutable` is only ever safe when the URL changes whenever the bytes do, and
# here it does: generate_site_v5.py rewrites every /shared/ reference to carry
# `?v=<md5[:8]>` of the file's own content, and a query string is part of the
# cache key. Change the file, the stamp changes, the URL changes, the cache
# misses. That is the whole contract.
#
# ⚠️ It was CHECKED, not assumed, before this file was written: 10,328 stamped
# references across the published tree and ZERO unstamped ones in any published
# HTML. The 18 unstamped `/shared/...` strings that do exist in the repo are all
# inside JavaScript COMMENTS showing people what to paste, and no browser ever
# fetches them. Runtime-injected scripts go through stamped() too.
#
# So before relaxing anything here, re-run that check. An unstamped asset under
# `immutable` is pinned in a child's browser for a YEAR, and no deploy can
# reach it — the worst kind of caching bug, because it is invisible to us and
# permanent for them.

# ⚠️ NEVER REQUEST A STAMPED URL BEFORE THE DEPLOY THAT PUBLISHES IT.
#
# Learned the hard way on the run that added this file. Verifying live, I
# polled `/shared/rum.js?v=<new stamp>` in a loop WHILE waiting for Cloudflare
# to publish. Every one of those requests missed, went to an origin still
# serving the OLD file, and cached that answer under the rule below — pinning
# stale bytes to the new URL, at that POP, for a year. `immutable` means what
# it says: nothing revalidates it, and no later deploy can dislodge it.
#
# Normal traffic cannot do this, because a Pages deployment swaps the HTML and
# the assets together — nobody asks for a stamp that is not live yet. Only an
# out-of-band check can, and CLAUDE.md tells every future run to do one.
#
# So verify in this order:
#   1. fetch the PAGE (it is must-revalidate, so it is always current) and read
#      the stamp out of its `__MRB_ASSET_V__` map;
#   2. only once that map shows the new stamp, fetch the asset — and add a
#      throwaway `&nonce=` so the check itself never populates the real key.

/shared/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The 3D studio is a Vite build: its filenames carry a content hash, which is
# the same contract by a different mechanism.
/3d/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ⚠️ There is deliberately NO `/*` rule here for HTML.
#
# The obvious thing to add is `/*  Cache-Control: max-age=0, must-revalidate`,
# to keep pages fresh. It is left out because `/*` also matches `/shared/...`,
# and the precedence between an overlapping wildcard and a specific rule is not
# something to be guessed at: if the later rule wins, that one line silently
# undoes both blocks above and the whole file becomes decoration. Pages already
# serves HTML as `max-age=0, must-revalidate` by default, so the rule would buy
# nothing and could cost everything.
